            <ArticleTitle>Response to vitamin D3 supplementation in obese and non-obese Caucasian adolescents.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>226-31</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1159/000343446</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND/AIMS" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Vitamin D deficiency is highly prevalent in obese children, and obese children tend to respond poorly to vitamin D supplementation. The objective of the study was to compare the response to vitamin D(3) supplementation (2,000 IU once daily for 12 weeks) between obese and non-obese Caucasian adolescents.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The study design was open label non-randomized. It was carried out at a single center. Eighteen obese adolescents (aged 12-18 years) and the same number of age-, gender- and season-matched non-obese adolescents received vitamin D(3) (2,000 IU/day) orally for 12 weeks. Total serum 25-hydroxyvitamin D [25(OH)D], parathyroid hormone, calcium and phosphorus were measured at baseline and at the end of the 12-week period.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The mean baseline 25(OH)D level was higher in the non-obese compared to the obese subjects (mean 28.9 vs. 25.2 ng/ml; p = 0.029). The increment in 25(OH)D levels following vitamin D supplementation was significantly lower in the obese adolescents (mean change 5.8 vs. 9.8 ng/ml; p = 0.019).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Higher doses of vitamin D are required to treat vitamin D deficiency in obese adolescents compared to their non-obese peers.</AbstractText>
